sSetType":"probability"},{"unit":"U3","topicCode":"3.1","skill":"4.D","q":"Two unbiased estimators are proposed for the same population proportion. Estimator A has a smaller standard deviation than Estimator B. Which statement is correct?","correct":"Estimator A is more precise because it has less sampling variability.","distractors":["Estimator B is preferable because greater sampling variability offsets possible bias.","Unbiased estimators for the same parameter must have matching sampling distributions.","Estimator A must reproduce the parameter for each sample."],"e":"Unbiasedness concerns the center of a sampling distribution, while precision concerns spread. With both centered at the parameter, the estimator with the smaller standard deviation is more precise."},{"unit":"U3","topicCode":"3.1","skill":"4.D","q":"A statistic T has a sampling distribution centered 4 units above the parameter it estimates. What does this indicate?","correct":"T is biased upward by 4 units.","distractors":["T is unbiased because its sampling distribution has a fixed center.","T has a standard error of 4 units.","T overestimates the parameter in every possible sample."],"e":"Bias is the difference between the expected value of an estimator and the parameter. A center 4 units too high indicates upward bias of 4; individual samples may still fall below the parameter."},{"unit":"U3","topicCode":"3.2","skill":"3.D","q":"A population has proportion p = 0.36. For random samples of size n = 100, what are the mean and standard deviation of the sampling distribution of p̂?","correct":"Mean 0.36 and SD √[0.36(0.64)/100] = 0.048","distractors":["Mean 36 and SD 0.048","Mean 0.36 and SD √[0.36(0.64)] = 0.48","Mean 0.64 and SD √[0.36(0.64)/100] = 0.048"],"e":"For a sample proportion, E(p̂)=p and SD(p̂)=√[p(1−p)/n] when the observations are suitably independent. Substitution gives √0.002304 = 0.048."},{"unit":"U3","topicCode":"3.2","skill":"4.E","q":"For a sample of size 40 from a population with 